From cc0910d7e7aec7f316227a6450826513ead7e971 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 09:57:36 -0300
Subject: [PATCH v2 1/7] hv: vmbus: convert ring buffer allocation to
 vmbus_alloc_buffer()

When opening VMBus channels under memory pressure or heavy buddy
allocator fragmentation, high-order allocations in vmbus_alloc_ring()
can fail. While an initial fallback to vzalloc() was proposed, that
breaks Confidential Computing (CoCo) guests (such as arm64 CCA and
Intel TDX without a paravisor), because set_memory_decrypted() only
supports physically contiguous, direct-mapped memory ranges.

With vmbus_alloc_buffer() available upstream, buffer allocations are
handled as a series of physically contiguous chunks down to order 0,
decrypted individually via set_memory_decrypted(), and mapped together
into a virtually contiguous range via vmap().

Convert vmbus_alloc_ring() and vmbus_free_ring() to use
vmbus_alloc_buffer() and encapsulate the buffer address, page pointers,
chunks and GPADL state in struct vmbus_buffer. This lets a ring still be
created when one high-order block is unavailable, and retains chunk-based
CoCo handling. Platform support still requires hardware validation.

Suggested-by: Michael Kelley <mhklinux@outlook.com>
Signed-off-by: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
