From fd445f46589264bca0cc01988686da9286f1cb82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 14:35:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH v2 6/7] hv: vmbus: retain buffer ownership until GPADL and
 page state are resolved

Converting rings and host-visible buffers to the chunked allocator
introduces a lifetime window that the previous contiguous allocation
never had. A buffer can now be mapped into userspace through UIO while
its GPADL is still being established, and a channel can be rescinded
while a create or teardown message is in flight. Freeing the backing
pages at the wrong point either leaks them to the host or hands pages
back to the allocator that are still host-visible or still decrypted.

Track each buffer with a channel-keyed retained owner that records the
allocation address, its chunk and page arrays, and the encryption state
that was actually reached. Owners move to a delayed reclaimer only after
three gates clear: the GPADL is resolved, the page state is known, and
the per-page references taken by UIO mappings have returned to their
baseline. A create or teardown with an uncertain outcome retains the
owner rather than freeing, so a partial post can never release pages the
host may still map.

Distinguish host rescind from local unload, since both previously set
the same channel flag and a rescind-based free could drop pages after a
successful-looking teardown that left its GPADL handle set. Revocation
now goes through an explicit channel-keyed entry point, and the
disconnected VMBus message path frees its allocated context.

In-tree migrated callers use descriptor-aware _owned entry points. The
previously exported allocator, free, GPADL establish, caller-decrypted
establish and teardown signatures are preserved through adapters, so
external consumers are unaffected.

KUnit coverage exercises the reclaim gate, the reclaim schedule gate,
host-revoke state, mapping references and repeated owner release.

Suggested-by: Michael Kelley <mhklinux@outlook.com>
Signed-off-by: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
